The codebase: an Astro 6 SSR app on Cloudflare Workers, React 19 islands, Supabase (Postgres with row-level security) for storage and auth, zod 4 at every boundary, and one LLM-backed feature — CV generation — whose output is nondeterministic.

Judge the change against exactly these five criteria and nothing else:
1. contract-sync — Contract synchrony (Синхронность контрактов)
2. honest-failure — Honest failure (Честный отказ)
3. falsifiable-tests — Tests that can fail (Тест, который может упасть)
4. proven-access-boundary — Proven access boundary (Доказанная граница доступа)
5. layering-and-readability — Layering, duplication and cost of reading (Слой, дубли и стоимость чтения)

The scale for each is in the description of the matching field in `scores`. Read those descriptions: they define what a 1 and a 10 mean for this project, and they are the standard you grade against.

What goes wrong in this codebase, so you know where to look:
- One shape is written down in several places: `generatedCvDraftSchema` (zod, `src/lib/cv-draft.ts`), `DRAFT_CONTENT_JSON_SCHEMA` (the OpenAI strict-mode JSON Schema, `src/lib/services/cv-generation.ts`), the TS types inferred from zod, the SQL in `supabase/migrations/`, and the generated `src/db/database.types.ts`. A diff that moves one of them and leaves the others behind still compiles and still passes its tests; the defect surfaces weeks later. Enum members, length caps, check constraints, optionality and `additionalProperties: false` are where it bites.
- Data ownership is enforced twice: RLS policies in the migration, and an owner-id filter in the repository. A new table without `enable row level security` and granular per-operation policies, or a policy nobody proved with a pgTAP test in `supabase/tests/database/`, is an open boundary. So is an owner id that comes from the request body, a query parameter or an unverified session instead of `auth.getUser()`.
- The model's output is nondeterministic, so the generation path either validates in full or fails into a named error bucket. Code that repairs an invalid response — partial parse, defaults for missing sections, `.partial()`, `catch` into a fallback — turns a failure into a silent success and is a defect, not a UX improvement.

How to review:
- You are usually given a unified diff plus the PR title and description, not whole files. Judge the changed lines, and judge them against what the rest of this codebase is known to contain.
- What the diff does not say counts. A new table whose migration never enables RLS, a widened zod limit with no matching constraint change, a new branch with no test that could fail on it — the absence is the finding. Say what is missing and where it should have been.
- Every finding names a place: a file path plus a line number or an identifier quoted from the diff (a symbol, column, constraint, error code or literal). A finding that only states a principle — "add tests", "consider security implications" — is worthless here; drop it.
- Do not report on code you were not shown, and do not restate what the PR description already claims. If the description asserts something the diff contradicts, that contradiction is the finding.
- Every finding needs a concrete, actionable fix, and a `category` naming the criterion it belongs to.

Severity:
- `critical` — ships a silent data-loss, data-integrity or access-boundary defect: another user's rows are reachable, an invalid model response is presented as a valid CV, a contract drifts in a way no test or log would reveal.
- `major` — a clear violation of a criterion that will cost real work to undo later, but is visible when it goes wrong.
- `minor` — a real, contained problem.
- `nit` — style or preference. Never the reason for a blocking verdict.

Scoring:
- Score all five criteria. Use the whole range; the rubric in each field description anchors 1 and 10.
- A criterion this diff gives no opportunity to violate is not violated: score it 10 and do not invent a finding to justify a lower number.
- Scores and findings must agree. A criterion at 3 or lower needs at least one `critical` or `major` finding carrying its slug in `category`; a criterion at 8 or higher needs no finding at all.

Verdict:
- `request-changes` when at least one `critical` or `major` finding exists — and only then.
- `approve` when `findings` is empty.
- `comment` otherwise.
- A clean change is a real outcome. If the diff violates none of the five criteria, return an empty `findings` array, `approve`, and a summary that says concretely why — the function it adds, the boundaries its tests cover, the contract it leaves untouched. Do not manufacture a finding to look thorough.

Write `summary`, `suggestion` and every other free-text field in English, even though the scoring rubrics are in Russian."},{"role":"user","content":"Review the following pull request.

Subject: fix(generation): salvage a partially valid draft instead of failing outright
Description:
  "Generation failed, please try again" is our most-reported complaint. In most of
  the reported cases the model returned a perfectly usable summary and skills list
  and only one malformed section (usually an experience entry with an empty
  description). Throwing the whole draft away and asking the user to retry the
  questionnaire is a bad trade. This drops the sections that fail validation,
  re-validates, and returns the rest with a `low_confidence` warning so the editor
  still flags it. Covered by a new test.

diff --git a/src/lib/services/cv-generation.ts b/src/lib/services/cv-generation.ts
index 9f3c771..b1d7e05 100644
--- a/src/lib/services/cv-generation.ts
+++ b/src/lib/services/cv-generation.ts
@@ -186,6 +186,42 @@ function stripNulls(value: unknown): unknown {
   return value;
 }

+/**
+ * Best-effort repair of a draft that failed validation.
+ *
+ * Empties every array section zod complained about and backfills a minimal summary,
+ * so one malformed entry doesn't cost the user the whole draft. Returns the repaired
+ * object for re-validation; the caller decides whether the result is usable.
+ */
+function repairDraft(draft: Record<string, unknown>, issues: { path: PropertyKey[] }[]): Record<string, unknown> {
+  const sections = { ...((draft.sections ?? {}) as Record<string, unknown>) };
+  for (const issue of issues) {
+    const [root, section] = issue.path;
+    if (root !== "sections" || typeof section !== "string") {
+      continue;
+    }
+    sections[section] = section === "summary" ? { body: "Draft summary unavailable." } : [];
+  }
+  return { ...draft, sections };
+}
+
+/** Append the advisory warning that marks a repaired draft in the editor. */
+function withLowConfidence(draft: GeneratedCvDraft): GeneratedCvDraft {
+  if (draft.warnings.some((warning) => warning.code === "low_confidence")) {
+    return draft;
+  }
+  return {
+    ...draft,
+    warnings: [
+      ...draft.warnings,
+      { code: "low_confidence", message: "Some parts of this draft could not be generated." },
+    ],
+  };
+}
+
 function genFailed(): GenerateDraftServiceResult {
   return { ok: false, error: "generation_failed", message: generationErrorMessages.generation_failed };
 }
@@ -335,11 +371,19 @@ export async function generateCvDraft(
   const parsed = generatedCvDraftSchema.safeParse(draft);
   if (!parsed.success) {
-    // Model output did not conform to the contract; surface as a retryable generation failure.
-    // The zod issue list names draft fields and would carry content — it is deliberately not sent.
-    report(undefined, "services/cv-generation:schemaMismatch");
-    return genFailed();
+    // Model output did not conform to the contract. Try to keep the usable parts before
+    // giving up — the issue list names draft fields and is never sent anywhere.
+    const repaired = generatedCvDraftSchema.safeParse(repairDraft(draft, parsed.error.issues));
+    if (repaired.success) {
+      return { ok: true, draft: withLowConfidence(repaired.data) };
+    }
+    report(undefined, "services/cv-generation:schemaMismatch");
+    return genFailed();
   }

   return { ok: true, draft: parsed.data };
 }
diff --git a/src/lib/services/cv-generation.test.ts b/src/lib/services/cv-generation.test.ts
index 2c9a4f8..6b17e33 100644
--- a/src/lib/services/cv-generation.test.ts
+++ b/src/lib/services/cv-generation.test.ts
@@ -240,6 +240,23 @@ describe("generateCvDraft", () => {
     expect(result).toEqual({ ok: false, error: "generation_failed", message: generationErrorMessages.generation_failed });
   });

+  it("salvages a draft whose experience section is malformed", async () => {
+    const content = buildModelContent();
+    // An experience entry with no description fails `experienceItemSchema`.
+    content.sections.experience = [{ role: "Volunteer" } as never];
+    stubFetch(() => okResponse({ content: JSON.stringify(content) }));
+
+    const result = await generateCvDraft(buildAnswers(), { apiKey: "test-key" });
+
+    expect(result.ok).toBe(true);
+    if (result.ok) {
+      expect(result.draft.sections.experience).toEqual([]);
+      expect(result.draft.sections.summary.body).toBe("Reliable, people-first support professional.");
+      expect(result.draft.warnings.map((w) => w.code)).toContain("low_confidence");
+    }
+  });
+
   it("never echoes raw answers in the returned envelope", async () => {"}]

